WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.299
Picture the detective from last time, with a new assistant.

2
00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:09.958
The archive now holds every clue the building ever produced.

3
00:00:09.958 --> 00:00:14.411
The assistant is sharp, and fluent in the archive language of an old job.

4
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:18.267
They ask the clerk for all reports where level equals error.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.618
The clerk comes back empty-handed.

6
00:00:20.618 --> 00:00:24.295
The assistant writes in the case file: no errors this week.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.132
In fact, there were hundreds.

8
00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:29.847
The clerk simply didn't understand the question, and said nothing.

9
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.881
That's the trap with a new query language.

10
00:00:32.881 --> 00:00:39.180
Old habits look right, and a wrong question often returns an empty answer instead of an error.

11
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.865
Today, the clerk is Dynatrace, and the language is DQL.

12
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.976
Start from something you already know, a shell pipeline.

13
00:00:46.976 --> 00:00:52.768
Each bar hands the lines to the next command, and you read it left to right, in the order it runs.

14
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.014
SQL, the common language for asking databases questions, works differently.

15
00:00:58.014 --> 00:01:01.557
SELECT comes first, and the database picks the order.

16
00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:05.233
DQL, the Dynatrace Query Language, is a pipeline again.

17
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:07.640
Top to bottom, in the order it runs.

18
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.952
Let's take the smallest table possible.

19
00:01:10.952 --> 00:01:14.711
The data command builds six records right inside the query.

20
00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:18.471
Everyone gets the same result, and the store scans nothing.

21
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.235
Six made-up visits to a web app: a path, a status code, and how many milliseconds each one took.

22
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:26.476
Add one line.

23
00:01:26.476 --> 00:01:31.525
Filter, status greater than or equal to 500, the server failures.

24
00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:33.467
How many records survive?

25
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:34.709
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:38.201
Two.

27
00:01:38.201 --> 00:01:43.963
The records flow down the pipe, and filter keeps only the ones where the test is true.

28
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.922
And notice the test: greater-or-equal.

29
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:50.404
Plain equality, in DQL, is a double equals sign.

30
00:01:50.404 --> 00:01:52.507
Remember that, it comes back.

31
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:55.026
Now swap the filter for a summarize.

32
00:01:55.026 --> 00:01:58.720
It groups the records by path and makes one row per group.

33
00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:02.268
Watch the four visits to slash gather into one pile.

34
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:05.795
Slash healthz and slash readyz each make a pile of one.

35
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.973
Each pile becomes one row.

36
00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:12.755
Four requests on slash, one error, 100 milliseconds on average.

37
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:15.583
Two details carry over to every query.

38
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:19.164
Each result gets its name with a single equals sign.

39
00:02:19.164 --> 00:02:21.712
And the by list sits in curly braces.

40
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:26.612
Add a computed column, the error percentage, and sort by its name.

41
00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:31.057
The worst path rises to the top: slash readyz, one hundred percent.

42
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:37.260
Read it out loud: take the records, group them by path, count, add a percentage, worst first.

43
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.387
Every line is one step, in order.

44
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.573
Now let's make the assistant's mistakes on purpose.

45
00:02:43.573 --> 00:02:45.756
A single equals sign in a filter.

46
00:02:45.756 --> 00:02:47.740
Without braces in the by list.

47
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.253
Both runs fail, with an error message.

48
00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.412
That's the good kind of mistake.

49
00:02:52.412 --> 00:02:55.088
It shouts, you read the message, you fix it.

50
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.271
Now real logs.

51
00:02:56.271 --> 00:03:01.544
Fetch the last hour of log records, and filter where log dot level is ERROR.

52
00:03:01.544 --> 00:03:04.527
Equality is right this time, double equals.

53
00:03:04.527 --> 00:03:05.637
What comes back?

54
00:03:05.637 --> 00:03:06.747
Pause and guess.

55
00:03:09.967 --> 00:03:10.965
Zero records.

56
00:03:10.965 --> 00:03:12.731
No error, no complaint.

57
00:03:12.731 --> 00:03:16.109
And the case file says: no errors this hour.

58
00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:16.680
Well.

59
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.138
The clerk didn't lie.

60
00:03:18.138 --> 00:03:21.330
There is simply no field called log dot level.

61
00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.037
The field is called loglevel, one word.

62
00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:28.271
A filter on a field that doesn't exist quietly keeps nothing.

63
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.360
So start every new query with limit 10, and read the real field names first.

64
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.644
Then add one command at a time, and check the row count after each.

65
00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:39.357
Zero rows is not proof.

66
00:03:39.357 --> 00:03:42.531
Check the field names before you write no errors.

67
00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:44.398
One more habit.

68
00:03:44.398 --> 00:03:47.877
In SQL, the database picks the order of the work.

69
00:03:47.877 --> 00:03:48.942
In DQL, you do.

70
00:03:48.942 --> 00:03:53.131
So what if we summarize first, and then filter on loglevel?

71
00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.942
Follow the records down.

72
00:03:54.942 --> 00:03:58.092
Before summarize, each record has its loglevel.

73
00:03:58.092 --> 00:04:03.387
After summarize, only the summarized fields exist: the namespace and the count.

74
00:04:03.387 --> 00:04:06.203
The filter asks about a field that's gone.

75
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:08.502
So filter first, then summarize.

76
00:04:08.502 --> 00:04:12.121
Now the query really counts error records per namespace.

77
00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:13.966
A few guardrails.

78
00:04:13.966 --> 00:04:18.564
The store behind all this, Grail, charges for the data a query scans.

79
00:04:18.564 --> 00:04:26.293
A month of logs costs real money on a paid environment, so start with the shortest window that answers the question.

80
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.282
Metric values come from a different first command, timeseries.

81
00:04:30.282 --> 00:04:31.868
There's no fetch for them.

82
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.620
And to match text without caring about capitals, use contains with case sensitive false.

83
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:43.015
Wrapping a field in lower stops Grail from using its index, so it scans far more data.

84
00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:45.708
So the moral of the story.

85
00:04:45.708 --> 00:04:52.342
A DQL query is a pipeline: records flow down, one command at a time, in the order you wrote.

86
00:04:52.342 --> 00:04:54.001
A loud error is a gift.

87
00:04:54.001 --> 00:04:56.020
A quiet zero needs checking.

88
00:04:56.233 --> 00:04:57.357
A quick check.

89
00:04:57.357 --> 00:04:59.926
This query hides two SQL habits.

90
00:04:59.926 --> 00:05:01.371
Can you spot both?

91
00:05:01.371 --> 00:05:02.575
Pause and look.

92
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.959
A single equals sign where DQL compares with double equals.

93
00:05:09.959 --> 00:05:12.427
And a by list without curly braces.

94
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.900
With this part, the watching is done.

95
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.249
What none of these tools asks is who else might be inside the building.

96
00:05:19.249 --> 00:05:22.680
Next, security, starting with the software supply chain.
